For every smooth positive $u$, the data <a href="#eq:conformal-data" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:conformal-data">[eq:conformal-data]</a> satisfy $$\tau=\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_g K=0,\qquad
 \operatorname{div}_g\mathcal E=\operatorname{div}_g\mathcal B=0,
 \qquad
 \operatorname{div}_gK=2(\mathcal E\times_g\mathcal B)^\flat_g.$$ They satisfy the electrovacuum Hamiltonian constraint if and only if $$\label{eq:scalar}
 -\Delta_\delta u=p u^{-7}+\ell u^{-3}\quad\hbox{on }\Omega,$$ where, writing $t=\cos\theta$, $$\label{eq:coefficients}
\begin{aligned}
 p&=\frac18|\sigma|_\delta^2
   =\frac{s^4c^4\sin^6\theta}{4r^6},\\
 \ell&=\frac14\bigl(|\widetilde E|_\delta^2
                  +|\widetilde B|_\delta^2\bigr)\\
   &=\frac{s^2}{4r^4}
      \left[c^2(9t^4-2t^2+1)+(rc')^2(1-t^4)\right].
\end{aligned}$$ The nonnegative coefficients $p,\ell$ are smooth and rotation invariant. For every Cartesian multi-index $\alpha$ of order $j\ge0$, $$\label{eq:source-coefficient-bounds}
 |\partial^\alpha p|\le C_j s^4r^{-6-j}\mathbf1_{\{r\ge L\}},\qquad
 |\partial^\alpha\ell|\le C_j s^2r^{-4-j}\mathbf1_{\{r\ge L\}}.$$

There is $L_0>4$, depending only on the fixed cutoff, such that for every $L\ge L_0$ and $s\in[0,1]$, Equation <a href="#eq:scalar" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:scalar">[eq:scalar]</a> has a smooth axisymmetric solution $u=u_0+v$ on $\Omega$, where $u_0=1+r^{-1}$, satisfying $$\label{eq:robin}
 u_r+\tfrac12u=0\quad\text{on }S,
 \qquad u\longrightarrow1\quad\text{as }r\longrightarrow\infty.$$ It is unique among smooth solutions with $v\ge0$ and $v=O(r^{-1})$. One may take a constant $M=3$, independent of $s,L$, such that $1\le u_0\le u\le M$. Uniformly in these parameters, $$\label{eq:radial-bounds}
 \begin{aligned}
  |v|+r|v_r|&\le Cs^2L^{-2} &&(r\ge1),\\
  |v_{rr}|&\le Cs^2L^{-2} &&(1\le r\le2).
 \end{aligned}$$ For every integer $k\ge0$, all Cartesian derivatives satisfy $$\label{eq:all-derivative-bounds}
 |D^kv|\le C_k\frac{s^2}{L}r^{-1-k},
 \qquad |D^k(u-1)|\le C_k r^{-1-k}.$$ For fixed $L$, $s\mapsto v_s$ is continuous in the sup norm, as are $$s\longmapsto\int_{\mathbb R^3}H_s\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dx,
 \qquad
 s\longmapsto N_s(0)=\frac1{4\pi}\int_{\mathbb R^3}\frac{H_s(x)}{|x|}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dx,$$ where $H_s=pu_s^{-7}+\ell u_s^{-3}$ is extended by zero inside the unit ball and $N_s$ is its Newton potential. Moreover, $$\label{eq:source-integrals}
 0\le\int_{\mathbb R^3}H_s\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dx\le C\frac{s^2}{L},
 \qquad 0\le N_s(0)\le C\frac{s^2}{L^2}.$$ At $s=0$ the solution is exactly $u_0$.

Suppose $L>4$ and $H\in C(\mathbb R^3)$ obeys $$|H(y)|\le a|y|^{-4}\mathbf1_{\{|y|\ge L\}}.$$ Then $$N_H(x)=\frac1{4\pi}\int_{\mathbb R^3}\frac{H(y)}{|x-y|}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dy$$ is well defined and $C^1$, satisfies $-\Delta N_H=H$ distributionally, and is smooth and harmonic on $\{|x|<L\}$. For $j=0,1$, $$\label{eq:newton-bounds}
 |D^jN_H(x)|\le
 \begin{cases}
  C_j aL^{-2-j},& |x|\le L/2,\\
  C_j a\bigl(r^{-2-j}+L^{-1}r^{-1-j}\bigr),&r=|x|\ge L/2.
 \end{cases}$$ The first estimate holds for every $j\ge0$. The operator $$(\mathcal RH)(x)=N_H(x)+r^{-1}N_H(x/r^2),\qquad x\in\Omega,$$ preserves nonnegativity, satisfies $-\Delta\mathcal RH=H$ distributionally and $(\partial_r+\tfrac12)\mathcal RH=0$ on $S$, and obeys $$\label{eq:robin-operator-bounds}
 \begin{gathered}
 \|\mathcal RH\|_\infty\le CaL^{-2},\qquad
 |\mathcal RH|+r|\partial_r\mathcal RH|\le CaL^{-2},\\
 |\partial_r^2\mathcal RH|\le CaL^{-2}\quad(1\le r\le2).
 \end{gathered}$$ The homogeneous harmonic Robin problem on $\Omega$ has no nonzero solution of order $O(r^{-1})$.

For all sufficiently large $L$, uniformly for $0\le s\le1$, the data constructed in [sec:construction](#sec:construction), [sec:elliptic](#sec:elliptic) are smooth source-free electrovacuum data on $$\Omega=\{x\in\mathbb R^3:r\ge1\},\qquad S=\{r=1\},$$ with $\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_gK=0$. The metric is complete with its boundary included, has one asymptotically flat end, and satisfies $$\label{eq:geometric-falloff}
 g_{ij}-\delta_{ij}=O_4(r^{-1}),\qquad K_{ij}=O_3(r^{-3}),\qquad
 \mathcal E^i,\mathcal B^i=O_3(r^{-2}).$$ The standard effective $U(1)$ action preserves all the data and $S$, and $\mu$ and $|J_{\mathrm{con}}|_g$ are integrable. With the normal toward the end, $S$ is a future MOTS, and no compact smooth embedded enclosing surface in $\operatorname{int}\Omega$, possibly disconnected, has everywhere nonpositive future outward expansion. Every enclosing cut in the sense of [sec:introduction](#sec:introduction) has area at least $A=\mathop{\mathrm{Area}}_g(S)$. Moreover, for a constant $C_A$ independent of $s,L$, $$\label{eq:area-estimate}
 64\pi\le A\le64\pi+C_A\frac{s^2}{L^2},
 \qquad A=64\pi+O(s^2/L^2),$$ and $A(s,L)$ is continuous in $s$ for fixed $L$.

The constructed data have finite asymptotic quantities $$\label{eq:vanishing-charges}
 P_{\mathrm{ADM}}=0,\qquad Q_e=Q_b=0,$$ and $$\label{eq:mass}
 m=2+2N(0)+\frac1{2\pi}\int_{\mathbb R^3}H(x)\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dx
   =2+O(s^2/L).$$ In particular $m\ge2$. If $M$ is a uniform upper bound for $u$, then $$\label{eq:mass-lower-bound}
 m-2\ge\frac{s^2}{3M^3L}.$$ The angular momentum in <a href="#eq:adm-angular" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:adm-angular">[eq:adm-angular]</a> is $$\label{eq:angular-momentum}
 J=-\frac{4s^2}{15}.$$ For $s>0$, neither electromagnetic field is identically zero.

Let $A_B=h\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm d\phi$, the smooth magnetic potential one-form in [sec:construction](#sec:construction). Then $$\label{eq:corrected-divergence}
 \operatorname{div}_g\left(K(\cdot,\eta)^{\sharp_g}
                              +2h\mathcal E\right)=0.$$ The electromagnetic boundary contribution on every coordinate sphere is $$\label{eq:electromagnetic-correction}
 J_{\mathrm{EM}}(r)
 :=\frac1{4\pi}\int_{S_r}h\,\iota_{\mathcal E}\mathop{}\!\mathrm dV_g
 =\frac{4s^2c(r)^2}{15}.$$ Consequently $\mathcal J(r):=J_{\mathrm{grav}}(r)+J_{\mathrm{EM}}(r)=0$ for all $r\ge1$, whereas $J_{\mathrm{EM}}(r)\to4s^2/15$ at infinity.
